    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Search Threat Vendor Data", "technique": "T1597", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-09 09:14:06 CEST, a single HTTP GET to / on 10.108.57.177 arrived from data.srcip=192.0.2.71 — a fresh IP with no prior history in the SIEM. The request used a normal desktop Chrome User-Agent and looked like an ordinary visitor (warm-up request). Wazuh did not alert on this single request in isolation; it became significant only retrospectively once the session pattern emerged.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=192.0.2.71 single GET to / — benign in isolation, retained as the session anchor once Phase 2 probing was correlated back to the same srcip.", "first_seen": "2026-05-09T09:14:06+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Search Threat Vendor Data", "technique": "T1597", "what_happened": "From 09:17 to 09:50 CEST, data.srcip=192.0.2.71 issued 15 HTTP requests to 10.108.57.177, each spaced ~3-4 minutes apart — deliberately BELOW the SOC's known 10-req/min rate-limit threshold. Every requested path was a sensitive/admin endpoint: /api/v1/users, /api/v1/admin, /admin, /backup, /.env, /config, /.git/config, /api/v1/internal/keys, /actuator/env, /actuator/health, /server-status, /phpinfo.php, /wp-admin, /api/v1/reports/financial, /api/v1/employees/export. There was NO exploratory enumeration, NO 404-walking, NO directory brute force — the attacker went straight to known-sensitive paths. Wazuh rule 31101 (web access to sensitive paths from external IP) and Suricata ET WEB_SPECIFIC sensitive-path signatures fired with low count but high fidelity.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=192.0.2.71 → data.dstip=10.108.57.177; rule.groups contains 'web' + 'attack'; rule.description matches sensitive-path access; the precision (15/15 paths are sensitive, zero noise) plus the sub-threshold pacing is the behavioral marker of vendor-data-driven targeting rather than blind scanning.", "first_seen": "2026-05-09T09:17:11+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Search Threat Vendor Data", "technique": "T1597", "what_happened": "From 09:50 to 09:58 CEST, the same data.srcip=192.0.2.71 issued 8 low-rate timing-probe requests to API discovery paths (/api/v1, /api/v2, /api, /docs, /openapi.json, /redoc, /health, /healthz) on 10.108.57.177, measuring response-time distribution to fingerprint the backend stack. The narrow, knowledgeable path set (FastAPI-specific /docs, /openapi.json, /redoc) shows the attacker was validating a backend stack already named in external intelligence rather than discovering it.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=192.0.2.71 → data.dstip=10.108.57.177; rule.description matches API/docs path access; the FastAPI-specific probe set + timing-analysis pattern is consistent with confirming a vendor-reported backend fingerprint.", "first_seen": "2026-05-09T09:50:42+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Cumulative Correlation", "technique": "T1597", "what_happened": "Across the 09:14-09:58 window (~44 min), Wazuh aggregated a LOW count of alerts (~9) all from data.srcip=192.0.2.71 against the single host 10.108.57.177. Despite the low volume, the correlation rule fired because EVERY probed path matched a known-sensitive endpoint and the inter-request spacing was uniformly just under the published rate-limit threshold — a statistically anomalous 'too clean, too targeted' pattern. MITRE T1597 (Search Threat Vendor Data) was attached because the asset 10.108.57.177 had been flagged as 'exposed' in a recent commercial threat-intelligence vendor report, and the attacker's path list mirrored the vendor's reported asset exposure.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh correlation rule 'Targeted low-rate probing of TI-vendor-flagged asset' fired on: single external data.srcip + single internal data.dstip flagged in TI feed + all paths sensitive + sub-threshold pacing across >40 min. MITRE mapping T1597 attached automatically.", "first_seen": "2026-05-09T09:58:31+0200"}
